# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
boethius-foreknowledge.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r1 has "□foreseen_occurs → ¬free"; boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r1.2 and boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r1.3 have "undeceivable" and "free → ◇¬foreseen_occurs"; they differ when only "there is freedom of the will" and "possibly, it is not so that that which providence foresees to be about to happen comes to pass" are true, which the first allows
boethius-foreknowledge.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered no, which keeps boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r1.2 and boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "there is freedom of the will" and "possibly, it is not so that that which providence foresees to be about to happen comes to pass" are true and "God can in no wise be deceived", "necessarily, that which providence foresees to be about to happen comes to pass" and "necessarily, that which providence foresees to be about to happen comes to pass" are false. Is S3, "Wherefore, if from eternity He foreknows not only what men will do, but also their designs and purposes, there can be no freedom of the will, seeing that nothing can be done, nor can any sort of purpose be entertained, save such as a Divine providence, incapable of being deceived, has perceived beforehand.", true then?
boethius-foreknowledge.md:1: argue/readings: S4 is read 2 ways: boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r1 and boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r1.2 have "◇¬foreseen_occurs → ¬sure_fk" and "sure_fk"; boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r1.3 has "free → ¬sure_fk" and "sure_fk"; they differ when only "there is freedom of the will" and "there is sure foreknowledge of the future in God, not uncertain conjecture" are true, which the first allows
boethius-foreknowledge.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S4 is read boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r1.3 with no modality; boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r1 and boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r1.2 as what is necessary or possible: its "can" does not say which it means
boethius-foreknowledge.md:1: argue/answered: S4: answered yes, which keeps boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r1 and boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r1.2, to: Suppose "there is freedom of the will" and "there is sure foreknowledge of the future in God, not uncertain conjecture" are true and "possibly, it is not so that that which providence foresees to be about to happen comes to pass" and "possibly, it is not so that that which providence foresees to be about to happen comes to pass" are false. Is S4, "For if the issues can be turned aside to some other end than that foreseen by providence, there will not then be any sure foreknowledge of the future, but uncertain conjecture instead, and to think this of God I deem impiety.", true then? [in 2 of 3 runs]
boethius-foreknowledge.md:1: argue/converse: boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r1 P2 (S3): the sentence makes the words after "no" the condition, but P2 has them follow from it: it reads "if necessarily, that which providence foresees to be about to happen comes to pass, then it is not so that there is freedom of the will"
